# **6. Why does this work?**

A palindrome has two identical halves when one half is reversed.

Using the slow and fast pointers,

we find the middle in one traversal.

By reversing only the second half,

we can compare corresponding nodes directly without using extra memory.

After comparison,

if every pair matches,

the linked list is a palindrome.

---

# **7. Java Code**

```java
class Solution {

    public boolean isPalindrome(ListNode head) {

        if (head == null || head.next == null)
            return true;

        // Find the middle
        ListNode slow = head;
        ListNode fast = head;

        while (fast != null && fast.next != null) {
            slow = slow.next;
            fast = fast.next.next;
        }

        // Reverse second half
        ListNode prev = null;

        while (slow != null) {
            ListNode next = slow.next;
            slow.next = prev;
            prev = slow;
            slow = next;
        }

        // Compare both halves
        ListNode first = head;
        ListNode second = prev;

        while (second != null) {

            if (first.val != second.val)
                return false;

            first = first.next;
            second = second.next;
        }

        return true;
    }
}
```

---

# **8. Time Complexity**

**O(n)**

* Finding the middle → O(n)
* Reversing the second half → O(n)
* Comparing both halves → O(n)

Overall:

```text
O(n)
```

---

# **9. Space Complexity**

**O(1)**

Only a few pointers are used.

No extra array or stack is required.

---

# **10. Important Interview Note**

This problem combines **three important linked list techniques**:

* **Fast & Slow Pointers** → Find the middle.
* **Reverse a Linked List** → Reverse the second half.
* **Two-Pointer Comparison** → Compare both halves.

> **Note:** Some interviewers may ask you to **restore the original linked list** after checking. In that case, reverse the second half again after the comparison.

---

# **11. One-line Note for Interviews**

**Find the middle using slow and fast pointers, reverse the second half, then compare both halves node by node. If all values match, the linked list is a palindrome.** 🚀
